  "final_answer": "I have all the information needed to answer the question. Let me provide the final response.\n\n## Final Answer\n\n**Bert's latest commit on GitHub** (https://github.com/google-research/bert) was committed on March 11, 2020, by jacobdevlin-google.\n\n### First 7 characters of the SHA:\n**`eedf571`**\n\n### What was changed in that commit:\nThe commit was titled **\"Merge pull request #1027 from iuliaturc-google/master - Add links to 24 smaller BERT models.\"** It was a merge commit (combining parents `cc7051d` and `8028c04`) that modified **1 file (README.md)** with **71 line additions**.\n\nSpecifically, it added a new announcement section at the top of the README titled **\"***** New March 11th, 2020: Smaller BERT Models *****\"**, which included:\n\n1. **Introduction** — Announcing the release of 24 smaller BERT models (English only, uncased, trained with WordPiece masking) referenced in the paper *\"Well-Read Students Learn Better: On the Importance of Pre-training Compact Models\"* (https://arxiv.org/abs/1908.08962).\n\n2. **Model dimensions table** — A matrix of model sizes based on hidden size (H=128, H=256, H=512, H=768) and number of layers (L=2, 4, 6, 8, 10, 12), highlighting recommended models:\n   - **BERT-Tiny** (2/128)\n   - **BERT-Mini** (4/256)\n   - **BERT-Small** (8/512)\n   - **BERT-Medium** (10/512)\n\n3. **GLUE benchmark scores table** — Showing results for BERT-Tiny (64.2), BERT-Mini (65.8), BERT-Small (71.2), and BERT-Medium (73.5) on tasks like CoLA, SST-2, MRPC, STS-B, QQP, MNLI-m, etc.\n\n4. **Fine-tuning details** — Information about batch sizes (8, 16, 32, 64, 128) and learning rates (1e-4, 3e-4, 5e-4, 1e-3) used, along with download links for the 24 models.",
